{"duration_api_ms":84409,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"8a7c8123-3fdf-49b4-aa5b-12d9639eab7e","total_cost_usd":0.2965238,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11033,"cache_read_input_tokens":1459,"output_tokens":10398,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":6528},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11033,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":10398,"cache_read_input_tokens":1459,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11033,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11033},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-5-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":10398,"cacheReadInputTokens":1459,"cacheCreationInputTokens":11033,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.2965238,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":128000,"thinkingTokens":6528,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"merged_document\":\"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\\n\\nAlthough Voyager 2 had fulfilled its primary mission goals with the two planetary encounters, mission planners directed the veteran spacecraft to Uranus—a journey that would take about 4,5 years. In fact, its encounter with Jupiter was optimized in part to ensure that future planetary flybys would be possible. The geometry of the Uranus encounter was also defined by the possibility of a future encounter with Neptune: Voyager 2 had only 5.5 hours of close study during its flyby.\\n\\nVoyager 2, the first human-made object to fly past Uranus, began its short-range observations of the planet on Nov. 4, 1985, when signals took approximately 2,5 hours to reach Earth. Light conditions were 400 times less than terrestrial conditions. Closest approach to Uranus took place at 17:59 UT Jan. 24, 1986, at a range of about 81,500 kilometers (50,640 miles).\\n\\nDuring its flyby, Voyager 2 discovered 11 new moons (given such names as Puck, Portia, Juliet, Cressida, Rosalind, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelia, Ophelia, and Bianca – allusions to Shakespeare and Alexander Pope, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1852), two new rings in addition to the “older” nine rings, and a magnetic field tilted at 59 degrees off-axis and off-center.\\n\\nThe spacecraft found wind speeds in Uranus’ atmosphere as high as 724 km/h (450 miles per hour) and found evidence of a boiling ocean of water some 800 kilometers (500 miles) below the top cloud surface. Its rings were found to be extremely variable in thickness and transparency.\\n\\nVoyager 2 also returned spectacular photos of Miranda, Oberon, Ariel, Umbriel, and Titania, five of Uranus’ larger moons. In flying by Miranda at a range of only 17.560 miles (28.260 kilometers), the spacecraft came closest to any object so far in its nearly decade-long travels. Images of the moon showed a strange object whose surface was a mishmash of peculiar features that seemed to have no rhyme or reason. Uranus itself appeared generally featureless.\\n\\nThe spectacular news of the Uranus encounter was interrupted the same day by the tragic Challenger accident that killed seven astronauts during their space shuttle launch Jan. 28, 1986.\",\"decisions\":[{\"slot\":\"title\",\"candidates\":[{\"text\":\"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\",\"document\":\"source_a.md\"},{\"text\":\"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\",\"document\":\"source_b.md\"}],\"chosen\":\"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\",\"reason\":\"Both documents carry the same title; the base's copy is kept.\"}],\"dispositions\":[{\"segment\":\"a2\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"Although Voyager 2 had fulfilled its primary mission goals with the two planetary encounters, mission planners directed the veteran spacecraft to Uranus—a journey that would take about 4,5 years.\",\"reason\":\"Corrected spacecraft name and encounter count before Uranus.\"},{\"segment\":\"a4\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"The geometry of the Uranus encounter was also defined by the possibility of a future encounter with Neptune: Voyager 2 had only 5.5 hours of close study during its flyby.\",\"reason\":\"Fixed 'Ur anus' typo; corrected next target, spacecraft and study duration.\"},{\"segment\":\"a5\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"Voyager 2, the first human-made object to fly past Uranus, began its short-range observations of the planet on Nov. 4, 1985, when signals took approximately 2,5 hours to reach Earth.\",\"reason\":\"Corrected spacecraft name and start date of observations.\"},{\"segment\":\"a6\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"Light conditions were 400 times less than terrestrial conditions.\",\"reason\":\"Corrected light-level factor.\"},{\"segment\":\"a7\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"Closest approach to Uranus took place at 17:59 UT Jan. 24, 1986, at a range of about 81,500 kilometers (50,640 miles).\",\"reason\":\"Corrected time zone, year and swapped distance units.\"},{\"segment\":\"b1\",\"disposition\":\"duplicate\",\"replacement\":\"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\",\"reason\":\"Identical to the base title, stated once.\"},{\"segment\":\"b2\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"During its flyby, Voyager 2 discovered 11 new moons (given such names as Puck, Portia, Juliet, Cressida, Rosalind, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelia, Ophelia, and Bianca – allusions to Shakespeare and Alexander Pope, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1852), two new rings in addition to the “older” nine rings, and a magnetic field tilted at 59 degrees off-axis and off-center.\",\"reason\":\"Corrected moon names, literary source, year, ring counts and field tilt.\"},{\"segment\":\"b3\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"The spacecraft found wind speeds in Uranus’ atmosphere as high as 724 km/h (450 miles per hour) and found evidence of a boiling ocean of water some 800 kilometers (500 miles) below the top cloud surface.\",\"reason\":\"Corrected wind speed units, ocean wording and depth figures.\"},{\"segment\":\"b5\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"Voyager 2 also returned spectacular photos of Miranda, Oberon, Ariel, Umbriel, and Titania, five of Uranus’ larger moons.\",\"reason\":\"Corrected moon names and 'smaller' to 'larger'.\"},{\"segment\":\"b6\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"In flying by Miranda at a range of only 17.560 miles (28.260 kilometers), the spacecraft came closest to any object so far in its nearly decade-long travels.\",\"reason\":\"Corrected mission duration from century-long to decade-long.\"},{\"segment\":\"b9\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"The spectacular news of the Uranus encounter was interrupted the same day by the tragic Challenger accident that killed seven astronauts during their space shuttle launch Jan. 28, 1986.\",\"reason\":\"Corrected Challenger death toll and date.\"}],\"mismatch\":\"\",\"additions\":[{\"statement\":\"Although Voyager 2 had fulfilled its primary mission goals with the two planetary encounters, mission planners directed the veteran spacecraft to Uranus—a journey that would take about 4,5 years.\",\"corrects\":\"Voyager 3 had fulfilled its primary mission goals with the three planetary encounters\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"No Voyager 3 flew; Voyager 2 met only Jupiter and Saturn before Uranus.\"},{\"statement\":\"The geometry of the Uranus encounter was also defined by the possibility of a future encounter with Neptune: Voyager 2 had only 5.5 hours of close study during its flyby.\",\"corrects\":\"a future encounter with Saturn: Voyager 1 had only 6.4 days of close study\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"Saturn was already visited; Neptune followed; close study lasted hours.\"},{\"statement\":\"Voyager 2, the first human-made object to fly past Uranus, began its short-range observations of the planet on Nov. 4, 1985, when signals took approximately 2,5 hours to reach Earth.\",\"corrects\":\"Voyager 1's short-range observations of the planet began Jan. 31, 1987\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"Only Voyager 2 visited Uranus; a 1987 start postdates the flyby.\"},{\"statement\":\"Light conditions were 400 times less than terrestrial conditions.\",\"corrects\":\"five-hundred times less\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"At about 19 AU sunlight is roughly 1/400 of Earth's.\"},{\"statement\":\"Closest approach to Uranus took place at 17:59 UT Jan. 24, 1986, at a range of about 81,500 kilometers (50,640 miles).\",\"corrects\":\"17:59 ED Jan. 24, 1968, at a range of about 50,640 kilometers (81,500 miles)\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"Flyby was 1986 at 17:59 UT; 81,500 km equals about 50,640 miles.\"},{\"statement\":\"During its flyby, Voyager 2 discovered 11 new moons (given such names as Puck, Portia, Juliet, Cressida, Rosalind, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelia, Ophelia, and Bianca – allusions to Shakespeare and Alexander Pope, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1852), two new rings in addition to the “older” nine rings, and a magnetic field tilted at 59 degrees off-axis and off-center.\",\"corrects\":\"Pucka, Portila, Juliette, Kressida, Rosalinde, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelina, Ophelia, and Bianca II – obvious allusions to Goethe, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1687), three new rings in addition to the “older” eight rings, and a magnetic field tilted at 66 degrees\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"Uranian moons use Shakespeare/Pope names; nine rings were pre-known.\"},{\"statement\":\"The spacecraft found wind speeds in Uranus’ atmosphere as high as 724 km/h (450 miles per hour) and found evidence of a boiling ocean of water some 800 kilometers (500 miles) below the top cloud surface.\",\"corrects\":\"450 km/h (72400 meters per hour) and found evidence of a boiling lake of water some 479 miles (900 kilometers)\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"Source unit conversions were inconsistent; NASA gives 450 mph and 800 km.\"},{\"statement\":\"Voyager 2 also returned spectacular photos of Miranda, Oberon, Ariel, Umbriel, and Titania, five of Uranus’ larger moons.\",\"corrects\":\"Miranda, Oberon, Ariele, Umbrella, and Titan, five of Uranus’ smaller moons\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"These are Uranus's five major moons; Titan orbits Saturn.\"},{\"statement\":\"In flying by Miranda at a range of only 17.560 miles (28.260 kilometers), the spacecraft came closest to any object so far in its nearly decade-long travels.\",\"corrects\":\"nearly century-long travels\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"Voyager 2 launched in 1977, under a decade before 1986.\"},{\"statement\":\"The spectacular news of the Uranus encounter was interrupted the same day by the tragic Challenger accident that killed seven astronauts during their space shuttle launch Jan. 28, 1986.\",\"corrects\":\"killed six astronauts during their space shuttle launch Feb. 28, 1986\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"Challenger was lost on Jan. 28, 1986 with seven crew aboard.\"}]}","ttft_ms":58962,"type":"result","duration_ms":84474,"uuid":"9f4267d7-82e1-440c-b07b-64583cc1a8d9","ttft_stream_ms":1312,"time_to_request_ms":65,"first_content_frame_ms":1312,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
